---
/* The Atlas. The map is the kit's; the argument is this archive's.
 *
 * WHAT WAS HERE, AND WHY IT CHANGED. Two hand-drawn SVG panels — Europe and
 * the world — with sixteen places, their coordinates typed into the page, on a
 * plain equirectangular frame that said of itself "longitude and latitude are
 * true, nothing else about it is meant to be". It was the only atlas in the
 * estate not on the shared map, and it looked nothing like the other seven.
 *
 * NOTHING IN IT HAS BEEN DROPPED. Every place, every word of every place's
 * note, every line and every caption is below. The two the old page carried
 * only in its table — Lourenço Marques and Johannesburg — are markers now,
 * which they always should have been.
 *
 * AND THE GAP IS STILL THE POINT. The old page drew two panels because one
 * frame could not hold Pomerania and the Indian Ocean. That distance was the
 * argument: two families that converge on four streets in Berlin, then break
 * in two directions at once — one son eight thousand kilometres south and
 * alive, his father and uncles three hundred kilometres east and not. On a
 * real map the distance is no longer a drawing decision; it is measured, and
 * the home view holds both halves at once so that the emptiness between them
 * is the first thing the page shows.
 *
 * THE LINES ARE NOT SOMEBODY'S GUESS. The kit drew every movement dashed and
 * violet and captioned it "Drawn by David on his own map. This archive has not
 * verified it." That is true of the archive it was written for and false here:
 * a deportation is the best-documented movement in this archive. Colour, dash
 * and provenance now come from the data, and the three red lines carry their
 * own dates. */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Atlas from '@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Atlas.astro';
import atlas from '../../public/atlas-data.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const u = (p) => `${base}${p}`;
/* The archive's own five, in its own colours, kept exactly as the SVG had
   them: --disp for Leibholz, --inf for Luwinski, --doc for Figueiredo,
   --accent for the murders. */
const cats = [
  ['leib', 'Leibholz — Szubin and Hammerstein',    '#8a2f2f'],  /* --disp   */
  ['luw',  'Luwinski — East Prussia',              '#6b5a12'],  /* --inf    */
  ['both', 'Where the two families are one',       '#55514a'],  /* --ink-soft */
  ['end',  'Where it ended',                       '#7a2e1f'],  /* --accent */
  ['fig',  'Figueiredo — Portugal and Mozambique', '#2f5d50'],  /* --doc    */
];
---
<Base title="Atlas"
  subtitle="Every place this archive can put a date to"
  lede="It runs from a Pomeranian market town to the Indian Ocean and back to the Atlantic, and the shape of it is the twentieth century.">

  <Atlas
    recordAtlas="https://daviddef.github.io/TheMap/place"
    dataUrl={u('/atlas-data.json')}
    cats={cats}
    bounds={[[-30.0, -12.0], [56.5, 36.0]]}
    homeLabel="Both halves, at once"
    placeholder="Szubin · Chełmno · Lourenço Marques"
    overlays={[['moves', 'The journeys and the deportations', '#8a2f2f', true]]}
    hint="Sixteen places and eight movements. The red lines are the deportations; the dashed red one is Oskar, who went to Theresienstadt first and reached Auschwitz nineteen months later."
  />

  <p class="small muted" style="margin-top:1rem">
    <strong>Red lines are the deportations.</strong> The dashed one is Oskar, who went to
    Theresienstadt first and reached Auschwitz nineteen months later. The faint dashed line
    running south is Kurt in 1933. The green ones are Ivone: four hundred miles of railway from
    Lourenço Marques, and at the end, Porto.
  </p>

  <h2>The places, with what happened at them</h2>
  <p class="small muted">The same sixteen rows the map is drawn from, because a list can be
    read straight through and a map has to be clicked.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Place</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {atlas.places.map(p => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong style={`color:${(cats.find(c => c[0] === p.cat) || [,,'inherit'])[2]}`}>{p.name}</strong></td>
          <td class="small">{p.what}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>What the shape of it says</h2>
  <p>
    Two families that began four hundred kilometres apart — Jewish at Szubin, Catholic at Allenstein
    — converge on <strong>one city</strong>, and inside Berlin on <strong>four streets</strong>. Then
    the line breaks in two directions at once. One son goes eight thousand kilometres south in 1933
    and lives. His father, his uncles and his aunt go three hundred kilometres east between 1941 and
    1944, and do not.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other half of the map has nothing to do with any of it.
    <strong>Lourenço Marques to Johannesburg</strong> is a railway line and four hundred miles; it is
    how Ivone arrived, and it is the only reason these two halves are on one page. She ended at
    <strong>Porto</strong>, which nobody else in this archive ever saw.
  </p>
  <p class="small muted">
    This page used to draw that distance itself, on a plain frame with two panels, because one
    hand-drawn picture could not hold both. <strong>The distance is measured now</strong> — and it
    is the same argument, made by the map instead of by the drawing.
  </p>
</Base>
